  "notes": "Falsetto-soul with a programmed base, and the entry's own stated model is \"the perfect combination of computerized instrumentation with a live feel\" from early-80s R&B — so the kit is quantised hard where Badu's and D'Angelo's are not, and the humanising is spent on velocity rather than on timing. The band is the sourced 76–101 straight with the backbeat on 2&4; the keys are the ones the entry names, major-mode more often than the lane average with the ballads in F♯ minor, B♭ minor and A♭ minor. Drums are \"programmed-but-humanised — a quiet, tight, gated kit with the snare mixed low and back, brushed or rimshot backbeats, continuous closed 16th hats\", which is the rimshot layer, the low snare velocities, the small offGrid window and the 16th hat carpet at a raised fill density with quiet velocities. The harmony is the family the research writes out — IMaj7–iii7–vi7–ii7–V7 — reduced to degrees with the sevenths moved into `extensions`, plus the ii–V movement it names; `maxChords` is 5 because that family is five chords long. Falsetto is stated as the melodic parameter: \"the top line spends most of its time above the tenor break, note-density is low, phrases are long and legato, and the lead is doubled an octave down\" — which is the high register, the density of 2–5, `articulation` legato and an `octave_echo` counter at −12. Bass \"sitting on chord roots with 8th-note motion and occasional slides between the root and the 5th\", with \"bass and kick locked to the same 8th grid\", is `mirror_kick` at a high root probability with a real glide. ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are the drum probabilities and velocities, the humanize values and every melody number — \"quiet\", \"low\" and \"long and legato\" are directions. ⚠ `confidence` is medium because the research rates the entry high-medium, and its own half-time reading of the ballads is marked [LOW-CONF]; that half-time subset is not encoded — this model keeps the straight 2&4 spine the Lane line states.",
